          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Cont
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cont
                  , that form a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+contexts"} category of contexts
                  of a
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Lang
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lang
                  that forms the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            %p
              constitute an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DependentTypeTheories
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi Cont
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi Lang
                    %mi LocallyCartesianClosedCategories
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    DependentTypeTheories
                    \\stackrel{\overset{Lang}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{Cont}{\to}}
                    LocallyCartesianClosedCategories
                    \\,.
          %p
            This (
            %a{:href => "#Seely"}> Seely, theorem 6.3
            ). It is somewhat more complicated than this, because we need to strictify the category theory to match the category theory; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %h2#ExtensionalIntensional Extensional vs Intensional
          %p
            There is an important distinction between
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional
            type theories and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
            ones. The meanings of these words is probably clearest when dealing with function types, such as an exponential
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
          %h3#extensional_and_intensional_function_types Extensional and intensional function types
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            is said to be
            %strong extensional
            if whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g\colon X\to Y
            are functions such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)=g(x)
            for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=g
            as elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            in the set-theoretic semantics, or more generally by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+objects"} exponential objects
            in the categorical semantics discussed above. The uniqueness clause of the defining assertion of an exponential object, i.e. that any map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\times X\to Y
            factors through a
            %em unique
            map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to Y^X
          %p
            By contrast, suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are interpreted by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+types"} data types
            in some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            is interpreted by some type of computable functions from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            is not extensional—equality of functions, as elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            = succeed "." do
              %strong intensional
          %p
            In type theory, extensional function-types generally come with both a “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            \-rule,” which specifies the computational behavior of a
            = succeed "-abstraction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x. t)(y) = t[y/x]
            = succeed "-rule,\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            = succeed "-abstraction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = (\lambda x. f(x))
            = succeed "-rule" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            = succeed "-rule." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
          %h3#quotient_types_and_exact_completion Quotient types and exact completion
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} Intensional type theory
            is also popular among adherents of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and especially
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-Lof"} Per Martin-Löf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            is often presented from this perspective.
          %p
            When viewing intensional type theory as a foundation for mathematics (rather than, say, a syntax for reasoning about computer programs), it is natural to view the types as representing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presets"} presets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
          %p
            If we then define “sets” to be types equipped with equality relations (sometimes called
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+sets"} quotient sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            of an intensional type theory has only weak exponentials (resp. dependent products), but that is sufficient to ensure that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+exact+completion"} free exact completion
            has actual exponentials (resp. dependent products). Note also that free exact completions always also validate
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
